Top,\nL1241@P11-12: Cells from PY broth culture. Bottom, Cells from PY\u0002glucose broth culture. See Fig. 11 for scale. 250 zyxwvutsrqp\nL1242@P12: MOORE, JOHNSON, zyxwvutsAND HOLDEMAN zyxwv\nL1243@P12: INT. J. SYST. BACTERIOL.\nL1244@P12: Because this species is an anaerobic, gram\u0002positive, nonsporeforming rod that produces\nL1245@P12: butyrate, it is placed in the genus zyxwvut\nL1246@P12: Eubacte\u0002rium. Cell shape and fermentation of cellobiose,\nL1247@P12: melibiose, and raffinose help differentiate E.\nL1248@P12: ramulus from other species of Eubacterium\nL1249@P12: that produce large quantities of butyric acid\nL1250@P12: from carbohydrates.\nL1251@P12: Euhacterium siraeum sp. nov. (Gr. adj.\nL1252@P12: si.rae’um [si.re‘uml sluggish [referring to the\nL1253@P12: relative inactivity of this organism in most sub\u0002strates tested]). Obligately anaerobic, nonspore\u0002forming, gram-positive rods, usually slightly\nL1254@P12: curved, occurring singly, in pairs or short\nL1255@P12: chains, sometimes in “V” or “flying-gull” ar\u0002rangements (Fig. 10). Some strains were motile\nL1256@P12: with one or two subpolar flagella. Cells were\nL1257@P12: about 0.5 to 0.6 pm wide by 1.3 to 6.0 pm long.\nL1258@P12: Colonies. After incubation for zyxwvut5 days in\nL1259@P12: RGCA roll tubes, colonies were 0.5 to 1.0 mm in\nL1260@P12: diameter, lenticular, translucent, and tan or\nL1261@P12: white. Larger colonies often had a dense center.\nL1262@P12: Surface colonies on BHIA-S roll streaks or an\u0002aerobically incubated BAP were 0.5 mm in di\u0002ameter, circular, entire, low convex, smooth,\nL1263@P12: shiny, and transparent to translucent. There\nL1264@P12: was no reaction on EYA and no hemolytic ac\u0002tivity. Three of eight strains tested did not grow\nL1265@P12: on anaerobically incubated BAP.\nL1266@P12: Cultural characteristics. There was no tur\u0002bidity in PY broth cultures without a fermenta\u0002ble carbohydrate. In the presence of a fermenta\u0002ble carbohydrate, broth cultures were slightly\nL1267@P12: turbid with a smooth sediment. Most strains\nL1268@P12: grew equally well at 37 and 45 C; there was\nL1269@P12: little if any growth at 30 C. Addition of 10 to\nL1270@P12: 30% (vol/vol) rumen fluid stimulated growth of\nL1271@P12: most strains.\nL1272@P12: Biochemical reactions. The biochemical\nL1273@P12: characteristics of 50 isolates of this species are\nL1274@P12: reported in Table 3. In addition, the type strain\nL1275@P12: FIG. 10. Eubacterium siraeum ATCC 29066.\nL1276@P12: Top, Cells from PY broth culture. Bottom, Cells from\nL1277@P12: PY-glucose broth culture. See Fig. 11 filr scale.\nL1278@P12: and seven other strains tested did not appear to\nL1279@P12: grow in adonitol, galactose, glycerol, inulin, or\nL1280@P12: sorbose. Small amounts of ammonia were pro\u0002duced from CM by some strains. Pyruvate and\nL1281@P12: gluconate were not utilized.\nL1282@P12: After incubation for 5 days, PY-cellobiose\nL1283@P12: broth cultures had a pH of 5.1 to 5.6.\nL1284@P12: Fermentation products. Fermentation prod\u0002ucts (average milliequivalents per 100 ml of\nL1285@P12: culture) were as follows.\nL1286@P12: From PY-cellobiose: Acetic acid (2.4).\nL1287@P12: Abundant hydrogen was produced from cello\u0002biose, fructose, or starch.\nL1288@P12: G+C content ofthe DNA. The G+C content\nL1289@P12: of the DNA is 45 mol% by T,,, for the type strain\nL1290@P12: and for the one other strain (All-17) tested.\nL1291@P12: Type strain. The type strain is ATCC 29066\nL1292@P12: (= VPI T9-50); it was isolated from human\nL1293@P12: feces.\nL1294@P12: Comments. All isolates we have of this spe\u0002cies were from human feces, and they include\nL1295@P12: S7A-55B, S9A-54, Sll-54, and T9-50.\nL1296@P12: Although there was no turbidity without a\nL1297@P12: fermentable carbohydrate, the fact that esculin\nL1298@P12: was frequently hydrolyzed when no turbidity\nL1299@P12: could be seen suggests that there was a slight\nL1300@P12: amount of growth in peptone medium. Cells\nL1301@P12: could be seen in Gram stains from PY cultures,\nL1302@P12: which also suggests that some cell multiplica\u0002tion may occur in the basal medium.\nL1303@P12: This species of anaerobic, gram-positive, non\u0002sporeforming rods is placed in the genusEubac\u0002terium because acetate or acetate and formate\nL1304@P12: (occasionally) are the sole major fermentation\nL1305@P12: acids. Phenotypically, this species most closely\nL1306@P12: resembles Clostridium leptum but differs from\nL1307@P12: C. leptum by actively fermenting cellobiose, by\nL1308@P12: not producing spores, and by having a G+C\nL1309@P12: content of 45 mol% as compared with 51 mol%\nL1310@P12: for C. leptum.\nL1311@P12: Clostridium leptum, sp. nov. (Gr. adj.\nL1312@P12: lep’tum thin, delicate [referring to the morpho\u0002logical appearance of the cells]). Obligately an\u0002aerobic, sporeforming, small, slightly curved,\nL1313@P12: gram-positive rods occurring in pairs or short\nL1355@P13: apparent growth and no fermentation acids in\nL1356@P13: pyruvate and gluconate media.\nL1357@P13: After incubation for zyxwvuts5 days in PY-maltose,\nL1358@P13: the pH was 5.3 to 5.8.\nL1359@P13: Fermentation products. Fermentation prod\u0002ucts (average milliequivalents per 100 ml of\nL1360@P13: culture) were as follows.\nL1361@P13: From PY-maltose: Acetic acid (2.0) and copi\u0002ous hydrogen.\nL1362@P13: G+C content of the DNA. The G+C content\nL1363@P13: of the DNA is 51 mol% by T,,, for the type strain\nL1364@P13: and 52 mol% for the one other strain (B8-26)\nL1365@P13: tested.\nL1366@P13: Type strain. The type strain ATCC 29065 (=\nL1367@P13: VPI T7-24); it was isolated from human feces.\nL1368@P13: Comments. Strains included in this species\nL1369@P13: are 58-21, B8-27, R7-49, S6F-42, T7-29, X4-45,\nL1370@P13: and M4-553 from feces or rectal contents and\nL1371@P13: M6-436 from descending-colon contents.\nL1372@P13: Esculin hydrolysis often took place, even\nL1373@P13: when there was no apparent turbidity or sedi\u0002ment in the culture.\nL1374@P13: Because this species is obligately anaerobic\nL1375@P13: and produces spores, it is placed in the genus\nL1376@P13: Clostridium. Phenotypically, it is unlike any\nL1377@P13: other described species in the genus. It most\nL1378@P13: closely resembles E. siraeum, from which it\nL1379@P13: differs in G+C content of the DNA, production\nL1380@P13: of spores, and lack of cellobiose fermentation.\nL1381@P13: ACKNOWLEDGMENTS\nL1382@P13: We gratefully acknowledge the support of this work by\nL1383@P13: the Public Health Service (National Institute of General\nL1384@P13: Medical Sciences grant 14604; National Cancer Institute\nL1385@P13: contract N01-CP-33334) and by the National Aeronautics\nL1386@P13: and Space Administration (contract NASA 9-12601).\nL1387@P13: We are indebted to Thomas 0. MacAdoo of the Foreign\nL1388@P13: Language Department of this institution for providing ap\u0002propriate specific epithets for the species described. We\nL1389@P13: especially appreciate the technical assistance of Loretta\nL1390@P13: Albert, Roger Cook, Maeve Crowgey, Pauletta Cumbee,\nL1391@P13: Arm Donnelly, Linda Hoffman, Dan Linn, Steven Lowe,\nL1392@P13: Carol Phelps, Christina Spittle, Barbara Thompson,\nL1393@P13: DiannP Wall, and others of our laboratory who performed\nL1394@P13: work on or for this project. We thank Elizabeth P. Cat0 for\nL1395@P13: help in compilation of the data, and M. P. Bryant for\nL1396@P13: suggestions concerning analysis of Desulfornonas pigra .\nL1397@P13: REPRINT REQUESTS\nL1398@P13: Address reprint requests to: W. E. C. Moore, Anaerobe\nL1399@P13: Laboratory, Virginia Polytechnic Institute and State Uni\u0002versity, P.O. Box 49, Blacksburg, Va. 24060.\nL1400@P13: LITEKATUKE CITED\nL1401@P13: 1. Bryant, M. P. 1974. GenusButyriuibrio, p. 420. In R. E.\nL1402@P13: Buchanan, and N. E. Gibbons (ed.), Bergey’s manual\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEmendation of Bacteroidaceae and Butyrivibrio and Descriptions of Desulfomonas gen. nov. and Ten New Species in the Genera Desulfomonas, Butyrivibrio, Eubacterium, Clostridium, and Ruminococcus (https://scispace.com/pdf/emendation-of-bacteroidaceae-and-butyrivibrio-and-3spebb4661.pdf)\nciteturn59view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 15; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn57view1\",\"pattern\":\"peptone\"}); Total lines: 1449\nL91@P1: Growth was greatly enhanced zyxwvu\nL92@P1: (4 +) zyxwvutsin PY-pyru\u0002vate broth, and pyruvate seemed to serve as an\nL93@P1: energy source. The best growth was obtained at\nL94@P1: 37 C, although some strains would grow at 30\nL95@P1: and 45 C.\nL96@P1: Biochemical reactions. Characteristics of 11\nL97@P1: isolates of this species are given in Table 1. In\nL98@P1: addition, the type strain and five other strains\nL99@P1: tested did not ferment adonitol, galactose, glyc\u0002erol, inulin, sorbose, or dextrin. None of the six\nL100@P1: strains produced ammonia in PY or chopped\u0002meat (CM) broth cultures. However, the type\nL101@P1: strain and two other strains tested produced\nL102@P1: ammonia from PY-urea broth cultures, and the\nL103@P1: pH was increased 0.3 to 0.7 of a pH unit. These\nL104@P1: three tested strains did not produce oxidase.\nL105@P1: None of the 11 strains reduced nitrate.\nL106@P1: The type strain was tested for metabolic ac\u0002tivity in several media. Little or no growth was\nL107@P1: produced in the absence of peptone in glucose\u0002mineral salts-biotin medium (5) reduced with\nL108@P1: cysteine, nor was H,S or H, produced. In pep\u0002tone-Trypticase-yeast extract medium with or\nL109@P1: without glucose, growth was poor, about 0.3%\nL110@P1: H, was produced in the head space gas, and\nL111@P1: small amounts of acetic and succinic acids and\nL112@P1: large amounts of H,S (as evidenced by immedi\u0002ate solid black color with the addition of satu\u0002rated ferrous sulfate solution) were produced.\nL113@P1: Formate and fumarate did not affect growth or\nL114@P1: hydrogen or H,S production. The reactions ob\u0002served in these media were unaffected by the\nL115@P1: addition of 20 mM MgSO, as compared with\nL116@P1: 0.07 mM MgSO, in the usual media.\nL117@P1: The higher level of MgSO, did affect the reac\u0002tions of all three strains tested in pyruvate\nL118@P1: medium. Pyruvate utilization increased from\nL119@P1: -\nL120@P1: FIG. 1. Desulfomonas zyxwpigra ATCC 29098. Top,\nL121@P1: Cells from PY broth culture. Bottom, Cells from PY\u0002glucose broth culture. See Fig. 11 for scale.\nL122@P1: an average of 53 to 80%, while hydrogen pro\u0002duction decreased from over 3% to less than\nL123@P1: 0.3% in the head space gas. Acetate production\nL124@P1: increased about fourfold. Probable increases in\nL125@P1: cell yield and H,S production were obscured\nL126@P1: because they were very high in both media. The\nL127@P1: high level of MgSO, improved lactate utiliza\u0002tion and markedly decreased hydrogen produc\u0002tion from lactate by the type strain, but little or\nL128@P1: no effect was demonstrated in lactate with the\nL129@P1: other two strains tested. These data indicate\nL130@P1: that the organism reduces sulfate but produces\nL131@P1: hydrogen in its absence, that high levels of H,S\nL132@P1: are produced from peptone and/or cysteine and\nL133@P1: from sulfate, and that energy metabolism is\nL134@P1: inhibited in the absence of sulfate as a hydro\u0002gen acceptor.\nL135@P1: The presence of cytochrome in cells of the\nL136@P1: type strain was demonstrated by J. F. Sperry\nL137@P1: by comparing the absorption spectra of dithio\u0002nate-reduced samples of broken, washed cells\nL138@P1: against air-oxidized samples. Absorption max\u0002ima were at 425, 527, and 556 nm and a mini\u0002mum was at 450 nm. Addition of 2 N NaOH to a\nL139@P1: cellular pellet showed